  <rdf:Description rdf:nodeID="commodity_dowlas_cb1b9b83531d579d5a6c9f504c85f5b00e1d199e145fd575a9916e22c59ec99181">
    <crm:P190_has_symbolic_content>a coarse kind of linen, much used in the 16th and 17th centuries (OED s.v. dowlas) | a coarse kind of linen, much used in the 16th and 17th centuries [named from Daoulas or Doulas, S.E. of Brest, in Brittany, like the associated lockeram from Locronan or Locrenan in the same vicinity] (OED dowlas) | dowlas, a coarse kind of linen (OED s.v. dowlas), lockram, a linen fabric of various qualities for wearing apparel and houshold use (ad. F. locrenan, from Locronan (lit. ‘cell of St. Ronan’), the name of a village in Brittany, where the fabric was formerly made) (OED s.v. lockram) 287, 289, 574 (see also lokeram) | (Doughlas, Bretysshe clothe, creste clothe or lokeram) dowlas, a coarse kind of linen (OED s.v. dowlas), lockram, a linen fabric of various qualities for wearing apparel and houshold use (ad. F. locrenan, from Locronan (lit. ‘cell of St. Ronan’), the name of a village in Brittany, where the fabric was formerly made) (OED s.v. lockram)</crm:P190_has_symbolic_content>
    <crm:P2_has_type rdf:resource="http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object"/>
  </rdf:Description>
